|
.\CLIENT_SPAWN_CHECKLIST.md:44:When building a `.tre` with `TreeFileBuilder.exe`:
.\docs\research\swg-tools-and-likely-studio-toolchain.md:11:The central pattern is consistent across 
the codebase: authors worked in high-level source formats and editor applications, generated loose 
SWG runtime assets under data search paths, validated those assets, then packed them into layered 
TRE archives consumed by the same `TreeFile` system used at runtime. The strongest local evidence is 
in `docs/data-pipeline.html`, the `sharedFile` IFF/TRE code, `MayaExporter`, `TemplateCompiler`, 
`DataTableTool`, `TreeFileRspBuilder`, and `TreeFileBuilder`.
.\docs\research\swg-tools-and-likely-studio-toolchain.md:17:Short-term conclusion: if the team wants 
a practical modern toolchain, prioritize three tracks. First, continue `swg-blender-plugin` as the 
DCC exporter/importer replacement for `MayaExporter`. Second, use or extend Utinni/Jawa Toolbox for 
live world placement, inspection, and reload workflows. Third, revive or replace the data build 
chain around `DataTableTool`, `TemplateCompiler`, `TreeFileRspBuilder`, and `TreeFileBuilder`, 
because those are the pieces that turn authored assets into shippable client data.
.\docs\research\swg-tools-and-likely-studio-toolchain.md:63:The runtime and the tools share many of 
the same libraries. That matters because a tool such as `MayaExporter`, `TemplateCompiler`, or 
`TreeFileBuilder` is not merely imitating the game format. In many places it is using the same 
serialization code or the same loader assumptions as the client.
.\docs\research\swg-tools-and-likely-studio-toolchain.md:128:3. `TreeFileBuilder` packs each 
response file into a `.tre`.
.\docs\research\swg-tools-and-likely-studio-toolchain.md:140:| `TreeFileBuilder` | 
`src/engine/shared/application/TreeFileBuilder` | Packs response-file entries into `.tre` archives, 
with optional TOC/file compression controls. | Build/release engineers |
.\docs\research\swg-tools-and-likely-studio-toolchain.md:149:Most important takeaway: 
`TreeFileRspBuilder` and `TreeFileBuilder` are the bridge from loose data to shippable archives. Any 
modern pipeline should preserve their semantics or intentionally replace them with compatible output.
.\docs\research\swg-tools-and-likely-studio-toolchain.md:346:| zlib | `TreeFileBuilder`, 
`DataTableTool`, content editors, and packaging tools link zlib. | TRE, datatable, compression 
paths. | Straightforward dependency; likely easiest to modernize if needed. |
.\docs\research\swg-tools-and-likely-studio-toolchain.md:361:| Core archive/data CLIs | Low to 
medium. `TreeFileBuilder` is mostly zlib; `DataTableTool` is zlib plus libxml2; `TreeFileExtractor` 
rides shared engine file/compression code. | These are the most realistic legacy tools to revive 
first. |
.\docs\research\swg-tools-and-likely-studio-toolchain.md:362:| Template compilers | Medium. They 
depend on Perforce API and PCRE/zlib, but the compile path is separable from Perforce 
submit/check-out behavior in source. | Candidate for a "no source-control" build mode if Perforce 
libs are missing. |
.\docs\research\swg-tools-and-likely-studio-toolchain.md:375:| WPS SWG Developer Tools | 
[swgnb.com/dev-tools](https://swgnb.com/dev-tools/) | VS Code suite for IFF viewing, datatable 
edit/compile/decompile, STF editing, and TRE packaging. | Strong candidate for modern 
data/localization/TRE workflows, especially where old MFC/Qt tools are too expensive to revive. Need 
compatibility tests against this client's `DataTableTool` and `TreeFileBuilder`. |
.\docs\research\swg-tools-and-likely-studio-toolchain.md:379:| SWG TRE Packager | [Marketplace](https
://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=WastedPotentialStudiosLLC.swg-tre-packager) | Builds 
TRE v0005 archives from selected files/folders with compression and validation. | Possible modern 
replacement or adjunct to `TreeFileBuilder`; must test archive compatibility and patch/search-order 
conventions. |
.\docs\research\swg-tools-and-likely-studio-toolchain.md:454:- Package a minimal test TRE with WPS 
TRE Packager and local `TreeFileBuilder`; validate both with local `TreeFileExtractor` and with a 
running client search path.
.\docs\research\swg-tools-and-likely-studio-toolchain.md:548:- Useful for independent validation of 
local `TreeFileBuilder`, `TreeFileExtractor`, and `StringFileTool` behavior.
.\docs\research\swg-tools-and-likely-studio-toolchain.md:571:- Possible modern comparison point for 
`TreeFileBuilder`, `TreeFileExtractor`, and WPS TRE Packager.
.\docs\research\swg-tools-and-likely-studio-toolchain.md:619:9. Pack `.tre` with `TreeFileBuilder`.
.\docs\research\swg-tools-and-likely-studio-toolchain.md:628:6. Eventually pack via 
`TreeFileBuilder`.
.\docs\research\swg-tools-and-likely-studio-toolchain.md:721:It should not be expected to replace 
`DataTableTool`, `TemplateCompiler`, or `TreeFileBuilder`. Those are build-chain tools with 
deterministic file outputs.
.\docs\research\swg-tools-and-likely-studio-toolchain.md:728:- Confirm `TreeFileBuilder` can pack 
archives consumed by the target client.
.\docs\research\swg-tools-and-likely-studio-toolchain.md:753:- Package a tiny archive through WPS 
TRE Packager and this repo's `TreeFileBuilder`; verify `TreeFileExtractor` and the client can read 
both.
.\docs\research\swg-tools-and-likely-studio-toolchain.md:762:- The major dependency buckets are now 
identified from project files and source: Win32/x86, MFC/ATL, Qt 3.3.4, DirectX 9/D3DX, DPVS, Miles, 
libxml2, zlib, PCRE, Perforce, Alienbrain, Maya SDK, ATI/NVIDIA helper libs, Oracle/ODBC, Mozilla 
embedding libraries, TinyXML, and parser-generation tooling.
